WEBVTT

00:00:12.290 --> 00:00:15.770
<i>Now we're 29,000 points 
behind second place.</i>

00:00:16.630 --> 00:00:21.800
<i>However, if Usuzumi gets a yakuman 
tsumo, it'll shrink to 21,000,</i>

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:29.670
<i>and if she gets a direct hit on 
Kiyosumi, it'll drop to 15,800.</i>

00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:43.180
<i>Should I stop blocking her?</i>

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:06.040
<b>The Nationals</b>

00:02:15.710 --> 00:02:19.130
Fourth Players' Match, Second Half

00:02:15.710 --> 00:02:19.130
East Hand 1
Bonus 2

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:22.930
Dealer: Haramura Nodoka

00:02:24.430 --> 00:02:27.430
North player: Usuzumi Hatsumi

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:33.230
<b>Hand 9: 
Sortie</b>

00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:30.560
Turn 6

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:36.630
Pon!

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:41.720
Pon!

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:07.390
<i>Usuzumi's yakuman...</i>

00:03:07.390 --> 00:03:11.250
<i>Of course, I could deal into it as well,</i>

00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:14.360
<i>but I'm sure our dealer is much 
closer to the abyss than I am,</i>

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:16.650
<i>since she doesn't see the yakuman threat.</i>

00:03:16.650 --> 00:03:21.580
<i>So! Get obliterated, 
inter-middle champion!</i>

00:03:25.780 --> 00:03:26.770
No tenpai.

00:03:27.590 --> 00:03:28.760
No tenpai.

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:29.520
Tenpai.

00:03:33.140 --> 00:03:35.510
<i>It's the shousuushii our captain talked about.</i>

00:03:33.140 --> 00:03:35.510
[Shousuushii - A suushiihou containing three each 
of three winds plus a pair of the fourth]

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:39.020
<i>That was quite the coincidence.</i>

00:03:40.340 --> 00:03:43.600
<i>Did she see the two calls and 
just drop out of the game?</i>

00:03:44.060 --> 00:03:46.990
<i>I guess that's possible.</i>

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:51.460
<i>In any case, Usuzumi will be the 
North player one last time.</i>

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:54.940
East Hand 2

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:54.940
Bonus 3

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:54.970
<i>I just need to hang in 
there and do my best.</i>

00:03:57.090 --> 00:03:57.880
Ron.

00:03:58.610 --> 00:04:01.880
Tanyao, two dora, plus 
three bonuses. That's 6,100.

00:04:04.190 --> 00:04:05.890
That's a 6,100 point direct hit!

00:04:06.250 --> 00:04:07.970
Usuzumi dealt into that hand.

00:04:07.970 --> 00:04:10.890
She's yet to win a hand in the second half.

00:04:11.450 --> 00:04:13.870
East Hand 3

00:04:14.410 --> 00:04:16.540
Turn 9

00:04:22.990 --> 00:04:23.990
Riichi.

00:04:23.990 --> 00:04:25.030
Chi.

00:04:42.510 --> 00:04:43.510
Riichi.

00:04:43.510 --> 00:04:44.360
Ron.

00:04:45.560 --> 00:04:47.080
3,900.

00:04:48.510 --> 00:04:49.500
Okay.

00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:53.460
<i>All right.</i>

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:55.590
<i>I've got a positive score again.</i>

00:04:55.590 --> 00:04:57.400
<i>Watch me, Sis.</i>

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:02.860
<i>I've finally come far enough to play 
in the same tournament as you.</i>

00:05:08.230 --> 00:05:11.610
<i>I don't want to take a 
single step backwards.</i>

00:05:15.160 --> 00:05:16.250
Tsumo.

00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:19.340
2,000. 4,000.

00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:23.960
Himematsu's Atago Kinue 
gets a mangan tsumo!

00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:27.060
She's consecutively won East Hand 3 and 4.

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:27.740
All right!

00:05:27.740 --> 00:05:30.350
Let 'em have it, Kinu!

00:05:31.250 --> 00:05:33.610
<i>I got these points from my sister.</i>

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:38.480
<i>I've got to defend them 
and increase them if I can!</i>

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:39.330
<b>East</b>

00:05:39.330 --> 00:05:39.770
<b>South</b>

00:05:40.210 --> 00:05:42.590
Second Half
 South Hand 1
 Dealer: Haramura Nodoka

00:05:41.310 --> 00:05:45.150
We begin the second half 
with Himematsu in the lead.

00:05:50.660 --> 00:05:53.190
The surprise here is Eisui's Usuzumi.

00:05:53.190 --> 00:05:56.000
She has lost over 25,000 points thus far.

00:05:56.510 --> 00:05:58.280
Could we say that the other schools'

00:05:58.280 --> 00:06:00.880
anti-Usuzumi tactics are working well?

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:05.150
No, she was in tenpai for 
a yakuman hand earlier.

00:06:05.150 --> 00:06:06.970
Once you've come this far,

00:06:06.970 --> 00:06:09.390
it's not a matter of tactics, but willpower.

00:06:09.390 --> 00:06:10.980
Well, well.

00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:13.510
So the Miyamori girl with the hair knot got her.

00:06:14.480 --> 00:06:16.120
It looks like it.

00:06:16.790 --> 00:06:20.400
But Hacchan's the North 
player again this hand.

00:06:21.210 --> 00:06:23.380
Turn 6

00:06:29.250 --> 00:06:31.380
<i>This is Usuzumi's last time 
being the North player.</i>

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:34.530
<i>If I can just get through this...</i>

00:06:37.240 --> 00:06:38.250
Riichi.

00:06:40.180 --> 00:06:41.540
<i>Again?!</i>

00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:51.930
Pon!

00:06:57.320 --> 00:06:59.270
<i>Even if I get a North, I'm not discarding it!</i>

00:06:59.270 --> 00:06:59.980
Kan!

00:07:02.190 --> 00:07:03.830
<i>Concealed kans are possible, too?!</i>

00:07:10.640 --> 00:07:14.950
<i>If Kiyosumi plays into her hand, 
we'll move up to second place.</i>

00:07:23.960 --> 00:07:26.170
<i>Why would I block her?</i>

00:07:26.920 --> 00:07:28.300
<i>There's a new dora indicator,</i>

00:07:28.300 --> 00:07:29.630
<i>and second place is the dealer.</i>

00:07:30.100 --> 00:07:32.200
<i>Kiyosumi declared riichi,</i>

00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:36.200
<i>and Usuzumi has all the 
East and North she needs!</i>

00:08:12.370 --> 00:08:13.490
<i>Nodoka...</i>

00:08:21.760 --> 00:08:24.230
<i>Come on, Kiyosumi. 
Play into her hand.</i>

00:08:25.750 --> 00:08:26.860
<i>I'll survive this!</i>

00:09:09.610 --> 00:09:10.450
Tsumo!

00:09:10.920 --> 00:09:13.940
That'll be 8,000 and 16,000!

00:09:15.010 --> 00:09:17.740
It's a shousuushii yakuman!

00:09:18.080 --> 00:09:21.490
Usuzumi has finally exploded 
into action in the South round!

00:09:21.490 --> 00:09:22.790
Kiyosumi, in second place,

00:09:22.790 --> 00:09:26.070
takes a painful hit to their score 
due to the dealer penalty!

00:09:29.120 --> 00:09:30.170
Sure.

00:09:30.590 --> 00:09:32.240
<i>She's unfazed?!</i>

00:09:34.710 --> 00:09:36.760
<i>Shousuushii...</i>

00:09:37.430 --> 00:09:39.780
Second Half

00:09:37.430 --> 00:09:39.780
South Hand 4

00:09:40.010 --> 00:09:41.390
Ron.

00:09:42.400 --> 00:09:44.520
3,900.

00:09:50.670 --> 00:09:52.640
The fourth players' match is over!

00:09:55.720 --> 00:09:57.400
Thank you.

00:09:57.800 --> 00:09:59.740
Good game.

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:01.620
Good game.

00:10:01.620 --> 00:10:03.620
Good game!

00:10:04.850 --> 00:10:07.160
The results of the fourth 
players' match are as follows:

00:10:07.750 --> 00:10:10.060
The four schools' scores 
remain virtually unchanged.

00:10:10.060 --> 00:10:12.130
Himematsu High School stays in the lead.

00:10:12.750 --> 00:10:18.380
Himematsu High School 
(South Osaka)
 Fourth player - 2nd Year
 Atago Kinue

00:10:13.170 --> 00:10:15.300
After taking over for her sister

00:10:15.300 --> 00:10:18.380
as the fourth player, Atago Kinue 
successfully protected their lead.

00:10:19.130 --> 00:10:26.020
Kiyosumi High School 
(Nagano)
 Fourth player - 1st Year
 Haramura Nodoka

00:10:19.270 --> 00:10:21.640
Last year's inter-middle champion,

00:10:21.640 --> 00:10:26.020
Haramura Nodoka, holds on to second 
place with a slight increase in points.

00:10:26.520 --> 00:10:28.210
Eisui's Usuzumi moves up

00:10:26.560 --> 00:10:32.020
Eisui Girls' (Kagoshima)
 Fourth player - 3rd Year
 Usuzumi Hatsumi

00:10:28.210 --> 00:10:31.520
from last place to third 
with a yakuman hand.

00:10:32.690 --> 00:10:40.030
Miyamori Girls' (Iwate)
 Fourth Player - 3rd Year
 Usuzawa Sae

00:10:32.710 --> 00:10:36.060
Miyamori's Usuzawa played admirably as well,

00:10:36.060 --> 00:10:39.330
but she was unable to add to their 
score and ended in fourth place.

00:10:41.250 --> 00:10:43.700
<i>After all that, we're in last place.</i>

00:10:43.700 --> 00:10:45.640
<i>I kind of saw it coming...</i>

00:10:45.640 --> 00:10:47.330
<i>But it still hurts.</i>

00:10:52.740 --> 00:10:54.000
Here I am!

00:10:54.320 --> 00:10:55.340
Toyone.

00:10:55.340 --> 00:10:57.490
Good job, Sae.

00:10:57.910 --> 00:10:59.860
Sorry. I finished in last place.

00:10:59.860 --> 00:11:00.840
No, no!

00:11:00.840 --> 00:11:05.470
We kept the point difference this small 
because you worked so hard.

00:11:09.790 --> 00:11:11.730
It's up to you now, Toyone.

00:11:12.060 --> 00:11:12.830
Yeah!

00:11:12.830 --> 00:11:14.490
I'll do my best.

00:11:20.490 --> 00:11:22.490
<b>Eisui Girls'</b>

00:11:23.450 --> 00:11:26.870
Guess I'll be going now.

00:11:26.870 --> 00:11:29.210
It's been a while since you've played, huh?

00:11:29.210 --> 00:11:30.650
Yes.

00:11:31.490 --> 00:11:32.620
Come to think of it...

00:11:32.950 --> 00:11:37.880
This is the first time we've entered 
the fifth players' match in third place.

00:11:38.730 --> 00:11:40.480
This time, I'll also...

00:11:40.910 --> 00:11:42.720
I'll be counting on you when I do.

00:11:45.220 --> 00:11:46.880
I'm off.

00:11:46.880 --> 00:11:47.430
All right!

00:11:47.430 --> 00:11:48.390
Good luck.

00:11:49.340 --> 00:11:51.670
<i>I'll be back, Komaki-chan.</i>

00:11:53.770 --> 00:11:55.130
Saki-chan!

00:11:55.130 --> 00:11:56.930
Your first appearance in the nationals!

00:11:56.930 --> 00:11:58.780
Give 'em hell!

00:11:58.780 --> 00:11:59.480
I will.

00:11:59.960 --> 00:12:01.280
Here I go!

00:12:07.060 --> 00:12:08.120
Saki-san!

00:12:09.060 --> 00:12:11.030
Good job, Nodoka-chan.

00:12:11.030 --> 00:12:11.850
Thank you.

00:12:11.850 --> 00:12:13.560
Good luck to you!

00:12:13.560 --> 00:12:14.330
Thanks.

00:12:20.950 --> 00:12:22.060
Here I go.

00:12:25.620 --> 00:12:27.050
<i>Go for it.</i>

00:12:28.810 --> 00:12:30.680
It's the sixth day of competition, 
and the third game.

00:12:30.990 --> 00:12:33.830
It's finally time for the fifth players' match!

00:12:47.620 --> 00:12:49.000
I didn't get us many points.

00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:50.160
I'm really sorry.

00:12:50.600 --> 00:12:52.710
What are you talking about, Kinu?

00:12:52.710 --> 00:12:55.460
You should be proud of yourself. 
You kept our lead.

00:12:55.460 --> 00:12:58.750
If you hadn't, it would've 
made my job a lot harder.

00:13:00.200 --> 00:13:01.890
All right, I'm off.

00:13:01.890 --> 00:13:03.240
Give it your best!

00:13:03.240 --> 00:13:04.720
Good luck!

00:13:13.060 --> 00:13:16.440
Why did Suehara-senpai say it'd be harder?

00:13:17.160 --> 00:13:20.450
Well, it's true that she's 
being a bit pessimistic.

00:13:20.450 --> 00:13:22.320
Our opponents are really tough.

00:13:23.140 --> 00:13:25.990
I personally have never 
sat down at the table with

00:13:25.990 --> 00:13:27.490
the intention of losing,

00:13:28.240 --> 00:13:30.940
but Kyouko always considers 
the possibility of losing,

00:13:30.940 --> 00:13:33.830
no matter how weak her opponents are.

00:13:34.420 --> 00:13:37.790
That's what makes her strong. 
That's who Kyouko is.

00:13:40.690 --> 00:13:41.670
<i>That's so nice...</i>

00:13:42.480 --> 00:13:47.470
<i>I hope my big sister will have 
such high hopes for me someday.</i>

00:14:12.330 --> 00:14:15.580
The battle for the top eight 
is drawing to a close!

00:14:15.580 --> 00:14:18.580
The fifth players' match is about to begin.

00:14:20.000 --> 00:14:21.940
Himematsu High School (South Osaka)
 Fifth Player - 3rd Year
 Suehara Kyouko

00:14:20.150 --> 00:14:21.940
Good luck.

00:14:22.250 --> 00:14:24.190
Eisui Girls' (Kagoshima)
 Fifth Player - 3rd Year
 Iwato Kasumi

00:14:22.610 --> 00:14:24.630
Good luck.

00:14:24.340 --> 00:14:26.070
Miyamori Girls' (Iwate)
 Fifth Player - 3rd Year
 Anetai Toyone

00:14:24.630 --> 00:14:26.210
Good luck!

00:14:26.210 --> 00:14:28.320
Good luck.

00:14:26.510 --> 00:14:28.440
Kiyosumi High School
(Nagano)
 Fifth Player - 1st Year
 Miyanaga Saki

00:14:29.880 --> 00:14:32.350
The first half has begun.

00:14:47.510 --> 00:14:49.490
Fifth Players' Match, First Half

00:14:47.510 --> 00:14:49.490
East Hand 1
 Dealer: Iwato Kasumi

00:14:52.620 --> 00:14:53.770
<i>Kan material...</i>

00:14:54.380 --> 00:14:56.420
<i>The next tile I draw is...</i>

00:15:01.710 --> 00:15:02.580
Pon.

00:15:13.700 --> 00:15:15.240
<i>Oh, my.</i>

00:15:15.240 --> 00:15:18.320
<i>I guess I'll use this one up, then.</i>

00:15:23.140 --> 00:15:24.380
<i>She's...</i>

00:15:26.900 --> 00:15:27.530
Ron.

00:15:28.710 --> 00:15:30.150
3,900.

00:15:32.320 --> 00:15:33.410
Yes.

00:15:33.820 --> 00:15:36.030
East Hand 2

00:15:40.910 --> 00:15:41.950
Chi.

00:15:46.210 --> 00:15:47.220
Tsumo.

00:15:47.220 --> 00:15:48.210
1,000. 2,000.

00:15:50.400 --> 00:15:53.090
East Hand 3

00:15:54.640 --> 00:15:55.560
Ron.

00:15:55.560 --> 00:15:56.940
2,600.

00:15:59.190 --> 00:16:01.060
Three consecutive wins!

00:16:01.370 --> 00:16:02.020
Yes!

00:16:02.020 --> 00:16:06.310
Himematsu's fifth player, Suehara, 
has won three consecutive hands

00:16:06.310 --> 00:16:08.710
and isn't giving the other players 
any time to unfold their game!

00:16:09.760 --> 00:16:11.350
<i>Is she in a hurry?</i>

00:16:12.430 --> 00:16:15.910
<i>She's in top gear and ready 
to make her getaway.</i>

00:16:19.810 --> 00:16:21.520
<i>My turn to be dealer...</i>

00:16:21.520 --> 00:16:24.060
<i>I'm not as happy about it as usual.</i>

00:16:26.350 --> 00:16:28.690
East Hand 4

00:16:35.490 --> 00:16:36.640
<i>Tenpai...</i>

00:16:37.220 --> 00:16:38.960
<i>I held back on winning too quickly</i>

00:16:38.960 --> 00:16:41.500
<i>due to the possibility of a draw,</i>

00:16:41.500 --> 00:16:43.420
<i>but I'll go on the offensive, even as dealer!</i>

00:16:43.800 --> 00:16:44.730
Riichi!

00:16:44.730 --> 00:16:45.680
Kan!

00:16:45.680 --> 00:16:46.750
<i>Kiyosumi!</i>

00:16:51.900 --> 00:16:52.990
Tsumo.

00:16:53.490 --> 00:16:54.680
Rinshan kaihou.

00:16:54.680 --> 00:16:55.610
8,000.

00:16:56.820 --> 00:16:58.070
It's a rinshan kaihou!

00:16:59.180 --> 00:17:00.620
She did it!

00:17:02.780 --> 00:17:06.320
<i>The rinshan kaihou is from the 
called kan, so I have to pay for it.</i>

00:17:08.230 --> 00:17:11.630
<i>I did expect it, but I guess 
Kiyosumi's fifth player is</i>

00:17:12.080 --> 00:17:15.180
<i>the type of player who can exert 
control under certain circumstances.</i>

00:17:16.070 --> 00:17:19.620
Rinshan kaihou is a yaku with 
a rather low probability,

00:17:19.620 --> 00:17:23.920
but Miyanaga is a player 
who often wins with them.

00:17:23.920 --> 00:17:29.230
Yes, her data is indeed 
hard to believe at first glance.

00:17:29.520 --> 00:17:31.840
South Hand 1

00:17:35.040 --> 00:17:37.360
<i>Even though I did win three hands in a row,</i>

00:17:37.360 --> 00:17:39.530
<i>that payment and the riichi stick I lost</i>

00:17:39.530 --> 00:17:42.160
<i>set me back 9,000 points, 
almost back to where I started.</i>

00:17:44.760 --> 00:17:45.960
Riichi.

00:17:55.010 --> 00:17:56.930
I'll chase you.

00:17:59.740 --> 00:18:02.060
Riichi if this passes.

00:18:10.450 --> 00:18:11.310
Ron.

00:18:11.730 --> 00:18:14.940
Riichi, ippatsu, one dora. That's 5,200.

00:18:14.940 --> 00:18:16.160
Okay.

00:18:16.930 --> 00:18:19.930
<i>My 4-sided wait lost to an edge wait?</i>

00:18:19.930 --> 00:18:21.700
<i>That's what I'd like to say,</i>

00:18:22.150 --> 00:18:24.870
<i>but this actually happens quite often.</i>

00:18:26.290 --> 00:18:28.830
<i>She chased with a hand like that?</i>

00:18:32.480 --> 00:18:35.730
South Hand 2

00:18:32.480 --> 00:18:35.730
Dealer: Anetai Toyone

00:18:37.670 --> 00:18:38.930
Riichi.

00:18:39.930 --> 00:18:42.930
<i>My hand isn't lost yet. 
I'll keep attacking.</i>

00:18:48.000 --> 00:18:51.830
If this passes, I'll chase your riichi.

00:18:54.920 --> 00:18:55.830
<i>Again?</i>

00:19:01.700 --> 00:19:03.700
<i>I don't like being chased by the dealer.</i>

00:19:05.080 --> 00:19:05.700
Ron.

00:19:07.220 --> 00:19:10.440
Riichi, ippatsu only. 3,900.

00:19:11.810 --> 00:19:15.260
<i>I got chased and dealt into 
her hand right away again.</i>

00:19:15.990 --> 00:19:18.120
<i>I'd like to think it's a coincidence, but...</i>

00:19:19.490 --> 00:19:22.410
South Hand 2

00:19:19.490 --> 00:19:22.410
Bonus 1

00:19:23.510 --> 00:19:26.110
Oh, a tsumo.

00:19:26.110 --> 00:19:30.560
1,300, 2,600, and the bonus, please.

00:19:31.160 --> 00:19:33.290
South Hand 3

00:19:33.860 --> 00:19:34.770
Kan.

00:19:35.360 --> 00:19:36.360
Tsumo.

00:19:36.940 --> 00:19:40.320
Rinshan kaihou, 500 all.

00:19:40.790 --> 00:19:42.790
<i>Rinshan kaihou only.</i>

00:19:43.920 --> 00:19:46.830
<i>I wonder if she's confident 
that she can definitely win.</i>

00:19:47.430 --> 00:19:50.350
South Hand 3

00:19:47.430 --> 00:19:50.350
Bonus 1

00:20:03.090 --> 00:20:04.260
Riichi.

00:20:05.100 --> 00:20:06.520
<i>Is she sure about this?</i>

00:20:15.560 --> 00:20:18.400
If this passes, riichi!

00:20:20.920 --> 00:20:22.900
She chases yet again.

00:20:23.220 --> 00:20:27.370
This is the third chasing riichi 
that Miyamori's Anetai has declared.

00:20:27.370 --> 00:20:31.630
All of them went after Himematsu's Suehara.

00:20:36.720 --> 00:20:37.670
Ron.

00:20:38.440 --> 00:20:40.340
That's 2,900.

00:20:40.340 --> 00:20:43.420
<i>Again?! This is the third time!</i>

00:20:43.420 --> 00:20:45.660
<i>At the regional preliminaries 
and in the first round match,</i>

00:20:45.660 --> 00:20:48.070
<i>she never did this...</i>

00:20:48.680 --> 00:20:50.060
Toyone!

00:20:50.060 --> 00:20:52.430
Yeah. She was successful.

00:20:52.430 --> 00:20:57.060
We were able to hide it until 
now thanks to all your efforts.

00:20:57.490 --> 00:20:59.780
She lost to chasing riichis 
three times in a row?

00:20:59.780 --> 00:21:02.190
And all of them were ippatsu.

00:21:02.190 --> 00:21:03.700
Senpai...

00:21:04.280 --> 00:21:06.160
<i>If she declares a chasing riichi,</i>

00:21:06.160 --> 00:21:08.800
<i>she can get a direct hit on 
whoever declared riichi first.</i>

00:21:09.230 --> 00:21:12.490
<i>Can something that strange 
actually be real?</i>

00:21:14.630 --> 00:21:18.200
<i>She didn't stand out that much 
in the previous matches...</i>

00:21:19.230 --> 00:21:21.770
South Hand 4

00:21:24.780 --> 00:21:27.840
<i>Still, I want to test it one more time.</i>

00:21:29.890 --> 00:21:31.140
Riichi!

00:21:31.140 --> 00:21:34.120
<i>If this doesn't work out, 
I'll keep quiet from here on.</i>

00:21:36.280 --> 00:21:38.690
Then, me too.

00:21:42.180 --> 00:21:44.400
If this passes, riichi.

00:21:51.250 --> 00:21:54.930
<i>The nickname she had back home was</i>

00:21:57.170 --> 00:21:59.490
<i>"Toyone Right Behind You."</i>

00:22:00.100 --> 00:22:04.340
<i>She strangles any player who declares 
riichi before her from behind.</i>

00:22:13.640 --> 00:22:14.970
Ron.

00:23:34.970 --> 00:23:49.900
<b>Next time:</b>

00:23:34.970 --> 00:23:49.900
<b>Friends</b>

00:23:36.370 --> 00:23:39.240
Pon, chi, pon, pon!

00:23:39.240 --> 00:23:40.600
Naked wait!

00:23:40.600 --> 00:23:42.940
Ma'am, did you know that 
in Chinese mahjong rules,

00:23:42.940 --> 00:23:45.730
winning from a naked wait gets 
you a yaku called "chanchuuren"?

00:23:45.730 --> 00:23:48.320
Seriously, you know so much.

00:23:49.900 --> 00:23:55.040
<b>Please Watch Again</b>
